These usages do not impose any invariant that tile.getChildByName("image") must be non-null; they are explicitly written to handle the case where the "image" child does not exist.

Furthermore, the XML loading logic does not guarantee that every tile element has an "image" child. Tiled maps can define tiles that either:
- use a single tileset image (no per-tile <image> elements), or
- have individual images for only some tiles.

Thus, for a general Element tile representing a <tile> element, it is completely valid for it to lack an "image" child, and in that case getChildByName("image") will reach the final return null; path. The field children can also be null if the element actually has no children at all.

Therefore, at this specific call site:

```java
Element imageElement = tileElement.getChildByName("image");
```

the method can indeed return null, and the existing code already defends against that with a null check. There is no stronger contract in the provided code that would prevent a null result for this invocation.
